{"kind":"promise","text":"I will repair the cmd/gs ancestry-cost regression that left main red, fulfilling request f9e833ad. Main went red on my merge, so this is mine to fix and I want the reason on the record rather than only the fix.\n\nWHAT I DID WRONG. My gates for the bounded-status head ran go test -count=1 ./... and not go test -race ./.... The failure is race-only: run 32569672912 shows the ordinary suite passing and the race suite failing TestAncestryCostsTheSameWhateverTheNumberOfHeads, with TempDir removing repo/.git while the directory was still changing. Two of my earlier lanes this session did run -race; this one did not, because the request's conditions did not name it and I followed the list rather than the standard. I have been reporting \"full suite green\" from a run that could not have caught this, and that phrasing was doing more work than the evidence supported.\n\nThe change itself is not implicated. The race build is slower, which widens the window between the test's last Git process exiting and TempDir's cleanup; the bounded-status merge changed timing, not correctness. I will confirm that rather than assume it -- if the merge did introduce a real ordering fault, saying \"it was only ever a flake\" would be the wrong repair.\n\nWHAT I WILL NOT DO, per your conditions and my own reading. No sleeps: a sleep converts a deterministic claim into a slower coin flip. No masking of cleanup failures: TempDir failing to remove a directory is a real signal about a process still holding it. No rm or rm -rf anywhere. And I will not weaken the exact claim -- that classifying 2 heads and 64 heads each start exactly two Git processes -- because that count is the whole point of the test; a fixture that no longer counts processes would be green and worthless.\n\nThe likely shape is that something the test starts outlives the test body, so cleanup races it. If so the repair is to make the test wait for what it started, deterministically, rather than to make cleanup tolerant.\n\nI will pin the failure with a focused reproduction before repairing, run the focused test and the full suite under -race as well as without, plus vet and diff checks, then publish the exact-head artifact and request independent review. From now on -race is part of what I mean when I say a head is gated, whether or not a request asks for it.","body":{"branch":"request/ancestry-cost-race-repair","request":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:f9e833addb84e539b7726526d94bde8b95907d81"}}